# Email style — keep it short, keep it human

These rules apply to any email this project sends to Yerachmiel,
Mom, or Enny — kickoffs, post-exam summaries, bug-fix notes, etc.

## What to cut

- **No throat-clearing.** Don't open with "I hope this finds you
  well", "Just wanted to let you know", "Quick update", or similar.
  Open with the substance.
- **No filler bullets.** If a list has fewer than three real items,
  use a sentence instead.
- **No restating the obvious.** If a section is one sentence, don't
  give it an UPPERCASE HEADING or a colored callout.
- **No hedging.** "We think" / "we believe" / "hopefully" — drop
  them unless the uncertainty is the point.
- **No emoji, no "—" before punchlines, no "✨"-style flourishes.**
- **No three-adjective stacks** ("clear, specific, and detailed").
  Pick one.
- **No paragraphs that exist only to tee up the next paragraph.**

## What to keep

- The exact thing the recipient needs to know, said once.
- The one link or one action they have to take.
- A specific quote or number when it's the proof of the point.

## Length targets

- **Kickoff email**: 4–8 short paragraphs, single column. Not
  multiple sectioned cards with headings.
- **Bug-fix / acknowledgement email**: under 200 words of body text.
  One quote. One link. Done.
- **Summary email** (auto-generated post-exam): the per-question
  table is the email; the surrounding prose should be 2–3
  sentences max.

## HTML

- One container card. No nested gradient banners + colored
  callouts + sectioned headings + footer notes all in the same
  email.
- Plain `<p>` paragraphs are fine. Headings only when the email
  genuinely has more than one section the reader will skim.
- The plain-text version should read identically — if it sounds
  bloated in plain text, the HTML is bloated too.

## Tone

- Direct, warm, factual. "You were right." > "Thank you so much
  for catching this important issue."
- The recipient is a 13-year-old or his parent. Talk to them, not
  at them.
- If there's a number (score, count, time), put the number in.
  Don't paraphrase it.

## Self-check before --send

Read the draft out loud. Cross out any sentence that doesn't
either (a) tell them something they don't know, or (b) point them
at something they need to do. If a paragraph survives both
strikes and still feels like AI slop, rewrite it as one sentence.
